            question: "A 60-year-old male is started on methicillin for a staph infection. Two weeks later, he develops fever, a maculopapular rash, and oliguria. Urinalysis reveals white blood cell casts that consist predominantly of eosinophils. What is the diagnosis?",
            options: ["Acute tubular necrosis", "Acute interstitial nephritis", "Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis", "Renal papillary necrosis"],
            correct: 1,
            exp: "Acute Interstitial Nephritis (AIN) is a drug-induced (Type I or Type IV) hypersensitivity reaction. It typically occurs 1-2 weeks after starting certain drugs (penicillins, NSAIDs, diuretics). The classic triad is fever, rash, and eosinophil-rich WBC casts.",
