            question: "A 40-year-old patient with congestive heart failure is placed on Spironolactone. This drug targets the principal cells of the collecting duct. What is the specific intracellular mechanism of action of Spironolactone compared to Amiloride?",
            options: ["Spironolactone blocks the ENaC channel; Amiloride blocks the aldosterone receptor", "Spironolactone competitively blocks the intracellular aldosterone receptor; Amiloride directly blocks the luminal ENaC channel", "Both drugs directly block the luminal ENaC channel", "Both drugs are Carbonic Anhydrase inhibitors"],
            correct: 1,
            exp: "Spironolactone (and Eplerenone) are steroid analogs that cross the cell membrane and competitively bind the intracellular mineralocorticoid (aldosterone) receptor, preventing it from transcribing new ENaC channels. Amiloride (and Triamterene) act directly from the urine lumen, physically plugging the existing ENaC channels.",
