            topic: "Cardiovascular", difficulty: "Normal", source: "Robbins",
            question: "A 55-year-old male with severe alcoholic cirrhosis presents with gynecomastia, testicular atrophy, and several blanching, central red lesions with radiating capillaries on his chest and face. These vascular lesions are directly caused by the liver's inability to metabolize which specific hormone?",
            options: ["Testosterone", "Estrogen", "Aldosterone", "Cortisol"],
            correct: 1,
            exp: "Spider angiomas (spider nevi) are benign vascular lesions consisting of a central arteriole with radiating capillaries. They are strongly associated with hyperestrinism (high estrogen levels). The cirrhotic liver cannot properly degrade estrogen, leading to spider angiomas, palmar erythema, and gynecomastia.",
